<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!-- virtualbase_snippet.urdf

     Heads up: this is NOT a robot you can run on its own.  It's a
     chunk of XML to paste into the top of our copy of the Atlas URDF
     when we start doing the stepping (milestone 2).  Don't bother
     with it for Tuesday.

     WHAT IT'S FOR:
     Right now Atlas is glued to the world - we just broadcast a
     world->pelvis transform in catch.py and he hovers there.  That's
     fine while he's standing still, but he can't step if he can't
     move.  The fix is to give the base six fake joints: three sliding
     ones (x, y, z) and three spinning ones (yaw, pitch, roll).  Then
     "where Atlas is standing" is just six more numbers in q, and the
     IK can solve for them like any other joint.

     Which sounds like it should make him float away, and it would -
     except we also add a task that says "the stance foot does not
     move."  That's what holds him down.  Pin one foot, let the solver
     shuffle the base around however it needs to, and swap feet when
     you want to take a step.  Kind of a cute trick honestly.

     STUFF THAT WILL BITE YOU WHEN YOU PASTE THIS IN:

     1. The Atlas URDF probably already has a <link name="world"> or a
        fixed world->pelvis joint somewhere.  Delete it.  A URDF can
        only have one root and now it's going to be ours.

     2. These six joints have to go FIRST in self.armjoints / the
        joint list, in exactly this order, or q lines up wrong and
        everything goes sideways in a very confusing way.

     3. Go into catch.py and delete the broadcaster.sendTransform()
        bit at the bottom.  Once these joints exist,
        robot_state_publisher publishes world->pelvis itself, and if
        we're also publishing it the two fight and the robot jitters.

     4. Check our KinematicChain.py actually handles prismatic
        (sliding) joints:
            grep -in "prismatic\|LINEAR" KinematicChain.py
        The HW5 version only knew about REVOLUTE and FIXED.  If ours
        is the same we have to add the sliding case, which is honestly
        easier than the rotating one:
            shift:    p = p + q * n
            Jacobian: Jv = n,  Jw = 0
        (makes sense - sliding along an axis moves the tip in that
        direction and doesn't rotate anything.)

     5. The prismatic joints need a <limit> tag or the URDF won't
        load.  The numbers don't matter much, they just have to be
        there and be big enough to not clip us.
-->

  <link name="world"/>

  <!-- slide along x -->
  <joint name="basex" type="prismatic">
    <parent link="world"/>
    <child  link="basexlink"/>
    <axis   xyz="1 0 0"/>
    <limit  lower="-10.0" upper="10.0" effort="0.0" velocity="0.0"/>
  </joint>
  <link name="basexlink"/>

  <!-- slide along y -->
  <joint name="basey" type="prismatic">
    <parent link="basexlink"/>
    <child  link="baseylink"/>
    <axis   xyz="0 1 0"/>
    <limit  lower="-10.0" upper="10.0" effort="0.0" velocity="0.0"/>
  </joint>
  <link name="baseylink"/>

  <!-- slide along z (can't go below the floor, hence lower=0) -->
  <joint name="basez" type="prismatic">
    <parent link="baseylink"/>
    <child  link="basezlink"/>
    <axis   xyz="0 0 1"/>
    <limit  lower="  0.0" upper=" 3.0" effort="0.0" velocity="0.0"/>
  </joint>
  <link name="basezlink"/>

  <!-- now the three rotations: yaw, then pitch, then roll -->
  <joint name="baseyaw" type="continuous">
    <parent link="basezlink"/>
    <child  link="baseyawlink"/>
    <axis   xyz="0 0 1"/>
  </joint>
  <link name="baseyawlink"/>

  <joint name="basepitch" type="continuous">
    <parent link="baseyawlink"/>
    <child  link="basepitchlink"/>
    <axis   xyz="0 1 0"/>
  </joint>
  <link name="basepitchlink"/>

  <!-- and this last one hands off to the real robot -->
  <joint name="baseroll" type="continuous">
    <parent link="basepitchlink"/>
    <child  link="pelvis"/>
    <axis   xyz="1 0 0"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- ...everything below here is the normal Atlas URDF, starting
       with <link name="pelvis">.  Don't touch any of it. -->
